@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+# US-39: Request and job limits
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Epic | Security requirements |
+| Requirement | MLE-SR-006 (availability; matches Member 3's section 3.9) |
+| Points / sprint | 5 / Sprint 2 |
+| Owner | Varnika (Member 2) |
+
+**Story.** As an administrator I want limits on how often and how much one user can ask of the service, so that one user cannot slow it down for everyone else.
+
+## The four limits
+
+| Limit | Value | Setting | Refusal |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Request body size | 10 MB | `WBMME_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES` | 413 `request_too_large` |
+| Request rate | 60 per minute per user, sliding window | `WBMME_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE` | 429 `rate_limited` + `Retry-After` |
+| Training jobs at once | Student 1, Data Scientist 2 (Admin 2, our assumption) | `WBMME_ROLE_GETTER` | 429 `too_many_jobs` |
+| Job run time | 300 seconds | `WBMME_JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` | job killed, run Failed (US-21) |
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- [x] Oversized body refused with 413 before it is read, even when not logged in; exactly at the limit is accepted, one byte over is refused.
+- [x] Chunked body refused with 411; malformed `Content-Length` with 400.
+- [x] Over the rate in any rolling 60 s gets 429 and `Retry-After`; limit is per user (per address when anonymous).
+- [x] A burst cannot be doubled across a minute boundary; refused requests are not counted.
+- [x] A user over their job quota gets 429 and no run is created; queued and running count, finished do not; other users never count; unknown role gets the lowest quota.
+- [x] A job over the time limit is stopped (real hung process, test_containment.py).
+
+## Files
+
+`training/ratelimit.py`, `training/quota.py`, `training/middleware.py` (add after AuthenticationMiddleware), `training/views.py`, `training/http.py`, `training/tests/test_limits.py` (33 tests, fake clock, no sleeping).
+
+## Evidence
+
+191 tests pass. Mutation check: changing `>=` to `>` in the limiter or quota, or the size comparison by one byte, makes a test fail.
+
+## Notes for the viva
+
+- Sliding window, because a fixed per-minute counter allows double the limit around the boundary.
+- Size is checked from the header first, so a huge upload costs almost nothing.
+- Queued jobs count towards the quota so a user cannot stack many jobs before the first starts.
+- Limitation: limiter and quota lock work within one server process; several processes need a shared cache and a database lock (INTEGRATION.md).
